Poverty maps provide granular estimates of poverty at the sub-national level in order to better inform the targeting of resources and support the design of interventions tailored to local needs (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "bedi2007more,elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 As household surveys are generally unrepresentative for small geographical areas,
 producing detailed poverty maps often requires using small area estimation,
 which consists of using statistical methods to predict poverty levels on the basis of household- or area-level characteristics.
 The basic procedure entails two steps:
 (1) using household survey data,
 fit a statistical model at the household or area level of some welfare measure (e.g.,
 poverty,
 income,
 or wealth) to household- or area-level characteristics,
 and (2) apply the model to census data available for all households or geographical entities to estimate poverty at the sub-national level.
 At a minimum,
 the procedure thus requires a household survey and data at the area level or a census to produce a sufficiently detailed poverty map.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature on small area estimation is rich and several variations on this basic procedure have been proposed.
 Unit-level models conduct estimation and prediction at the household level,
 assuming a linear relationship between the welfare measure and covariates 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hentschel1998combining,elbers2003micro,molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Unit-level models are not well-suited to situations where the survey and census data correspond to different years,
 which is often the case in developing countries where censuses are conducted infrequently.
 Area-level models represent a feasible alternative for periods where a recent census is not available that similarly relies on linear functional forms,
 but conduct estimation and prediction using only aggregate data for the geographical entities of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "fay1979estimates,torabi2014small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Unit-context models have been suggested alternative and are characterized by an estimation stage wherein household-level measures are modeled exclusively as a linear function of area-level characteristics 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "nguyen2012method,lange2018small,masaki2020small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent developments in poverty mapping have focused on the application of machine learning to remotely-sensed data,
 largely in response to the issue of out-of-date census information.
 These approaches also use survey-derived welfare measures,
 but are distinguished by a first stage where a machine-learning model is fit to remotely-sensed covariates (e.g.,
 data derived from satellite imagery or call detail records) rather than census-based covariates.
 For example,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 matched data from several remotely-sensed data sources to survey-based measures of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

village
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 wealth for 56 countries,
 and then fit a prediction model using gradient boosting machines.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Their remotely-sensed data includes high-resolution satellite imagery,
 mobile phone data,
 topographic maps,
 and connectivity data from Facebook.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 They then applied the model to the populated surface of all 135 low- and middle-income countries to develop granular poverty maps for each country.
 Similar approaches have been used for individual countries,
 including Rwanda 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Senegal 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Bangladesh 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and Belize 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hersh2021open"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 among others.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for examples of other recent applications.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While often subsumed under the term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

machine learning,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 this modern approach to poverty mapping actually consists of several practices that differ from more traditional approaches.
 Most obviously,
 the modern approach substitutes non-parametric statistical methods for the parametric approaches traditionally used for poverty mapping.
 In addition,
 the modern approach relies heavily on remotely-sensed covariates rather than covariates derived from census data.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Nevertheless,
 traditional area-level methods have made considerable use of remotely sensed covariates and often rely on using data available at the area-level,
 including administrative data (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "seitz2019they"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an application using publicly available geospatial data).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Finally,
 poverty maps produced with machine-learning methods generally share a common validation procedure.
 The key feature of this procedure is that model performance is assessed by calculating the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression of the observed poverty measures on the estimated poverty measures for the same geographical units.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For examples of the above procedure,
 see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Note that a few studies use a similar procedure,
 but fit the machine-learning model to household-level data and then aggregate the model's predictions to the geographic unit of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting,hersh2021open,aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The poverty measures used in this procedure are most often direct,
 sample-based estimates rather than the true poverty measures for the regions of interest.
 While direct estimates are unbiased,
 they can be imprecise estimates of true poverty measures,
 meaning that it is unclear whether the validation procedure is informative of actual model performance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In addition,
 we are often interested in how well the predictions perform in all areas,
 not on aggregate.
 Consequently,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 measure hides the heterogeneity across areas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this paper,
 we provide a more rigorous assessment of the performance of the modern approach to poverty mapping.
 Our approach consists of constructing a pseudo-census (hereafter 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

census
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) that we use to conduct several simulation experiments.
 Our experiments entail repeatedly drawing survey samples from the census where each sample produces direct poverty estimates for the sampled geographic areas.
 With each sample we obtain small area estimates using traditional and machine learning based methods.
 The presence of the census means that we observe the ``true'' poverty measure corresponding to the direct estimate.
 We use these true poverty measures to gain insight not only into the credibility of model validation based on direct estimates,
 but also the performance of machine-learning methods when validated against the true poverty measures.
 Given that population censuses rarely collect detailed data on income or expenditures,
 we construct our census from a large-scale household survey conducted in Mexico:
 the Mexican Intercensal Survey of 2015.
 The Mexican Intracensal Survey represents our population and it contains an income measure that yields the true poverty values that we wish to estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast to other papers which focus on the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 measure,
 which omits much of the within area accuracy of our estimates,
 we focus on the mean squared error.
 We argue that the mean squared error is a better measure for understanding the strength of the association between the poverty estimates and reference measures.
 In addition,
 we argue that the ultimate concern is to understand how different methodological choices affect poverty by influencing the efficiency of targeting.
 We therefore propose a simple targeting experiment through which we examine the relative ability of alternative mapping methods to alleviate poverty in the context of the Mexican data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of our simulations can be summarized as follows.
 First,
 when assessing model performance based on the true poverty rates using the mean squared error,
 we find that the quality of the data used for modeling is crucial.
 Hence,
 models that rely on publicly available remotely-sensed covariates are unlikely to yield estimates that are of similar quality to models that include census-based covariates.
 We find that these performance issues are largely due to the limited predictive power of remotely-sensed covariates relative to census-based covariates.
 When comparing machine learning based methods to traditional small area methods using similar data,
 then machine learning methods perform just as well or better to traditional methods.
 Finally,
 our targeting simulations which rely on accurate rankings of locations show that our machine-learning implementations perform as well as more traditional poverty mapping methods only when relying on similar data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our paper builds on the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 who similarly used the Mexican Intercensal Survey to examine the performance of several poverty mapping methods.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 focused exclusively on traditional poverty mapping approaches and did not consider the performance of machine-learning methods.
 While 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 consider the performance of machine-learning approaches,
 their implementation only uses census-based covariates and thus does not examine the performance of the standard implementation that relies on remotely-sensed covariates.
 Importantly,
 we are not the first paper to evaluate the performance of machine-learning methods relative to census data.
 While 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 validate their models relative to censuses,
 both papers use the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric and focus on wealth estimates rather than poverty.
 Finally,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "vanderweide2022how"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 examine how well machine-learning methods using remotely-sensed data can reproduce poverty maps estimated using census data.
 They do not,
 however,
 compare their estimates to a ground truth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper then represents the first attempt to rigorously assess the performance of modern poverty mapping methods relative to true poverty rates.
 In what follows,
