name "sec:methods"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section describes the methods we use in our poverty-mapping simulations.
 The first subsection discusses machine learning,
 with a special focus on gradient boosting,
 as it is among the most popular machine-learning methods for poverty mapping.
 The second subsection then discusses more traditional approaches to poverty mapping,
 which we use as the benchmark in our performance assessment of the machine-learning methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Machine learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of machine learning is to develop high-performance algorithms for prediction,
 classification,
 and clustering/grouping tasks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "varian2014big,athey2018impact,athey2019machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Such tasks can be divided into supervised and unsupervised learning problems.
 While unsupervised learning focuses on identifying clusters of observations that are similar in terms of their features,
 supervised learning uses a set of features to predict some outcome of interest.
 Supervised learning can be further divided into regression and classification tasks,
 where regression is concerned with predicting continuous outcomes and classification focuses on categorical outcomes.
 While there are many machine-learning methods used for regression and classification tasks (e.g.,
 lasso,
 random forests,
 or support vector machines),
 the following outlines some key ideas by focusing on one of the leading methods:
 gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Originally developed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "friedman2001greedy"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 gradient boosting machines are a family of machine-learning techniques that combine a large number of weak learners to form a stronger ensemble prediction.
 Unlike some common ensemble techniques (e.g.,
 random forests) that simply average models in the ensemble,
 gradient boosting adds models to the ensemble sequentially by fitting new weak learners to the negative gradient of some chosen loss function.
 With the classic squared-error loss function,
 this amounts to sequentially fitting new models to the current residuals of the ensemble.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "natekin2013gradient"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an accessible introduction to gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) is a particularly popular implementation of gradient boosting that uses classification and regression trees as the base models or weak learners.
 The popularity of XGBoost is due to the fact that it is fast,
 scalable,
 and has been shown to outperform competing methods across a wide range of problems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Classification and regression trees are based on a sequential binary splitting of the covariate space that serves to partition the data into subsamples that are increasingly homogeneous in terms of the outcome variable.
 A tree begins with a single root node that is split into two child nodes according to some decision rule.
 A decision rule pertains to a single explanatory variable and observations are assigned to the child nodes depending on whether they meet some condition associated with the explanatory variable.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For example,
 for a continuous explanatory variable,
 one determines whether a given observation falls above or below some numeric cutoff.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The child nodes can be further split into new child nodes based on different decision rules that involve other explanatory variables and cutoff points.
 Any child node that is not further split is referred to as a terminal node or leaf,
 and each leaf is associated with a parameter that provides a prediction for the subsample associated with that leaf.
 For any given observation,
 the ensemble prediction used by XGBoost is the sum of that observation's predictions across all trees in the ensemble.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To fix ideas,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 denote the outcome of interest (e.g.,
 direct estimates of poverty rates) for observation or entity 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote a vector of explanatory variables.
 In line with the above,
 the predicted value of the outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 is given by the sum of predictions across the individual trees in the ensemble:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{p}=\sum_{t}f_{t}(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the trees are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 gives the prediction of a given tree as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 To build the trees used in the model,
 XGBoost minimizes the following regularized objective function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i}^{p})+\sum_{t}r(f_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i}^{p})$
\end_inset

 is the loss associated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 observation and 
\begin_inset Formula $r(f_{t})$
\end_inset

 is a regularization term that serves to mitigate overfitting.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For an in-depth discussion and description of the XGBoost algorithm refer to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In our implementation,
 we use the classic squared-error loss such that 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i}^{p})=(y_{i}^{d}-y_{i}^{p})^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
XGBoost uses the following specification of the regularization term:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r(f_{t})=\gamma M_{t}+\frac{1}{2}\lambda\sum_{j}m_{tj}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of leaves in tree 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

 is the prediction associated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{th}$
\end_inset

 leaf in a given tree,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 are hyperparameters that must be chosen by the user.
 The regularization term serves to mitigate overfitting by penalizing complicated trees and smoothing the predictions associated with each tree.
 That is,
 the method relies on a large number of weak learners and the regularization term helps control the complexity of the learners.
 It is important to note that the above specification of the regularization term is only one of many possible specifications,
 though 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 claim that it is simpler than the leading alternatives and easier to parallelize.
 Further note that when the hyperparameters are set to zero,
 the objective function becomes equivalent to that used in traditional gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
XGBoost requires the specification of several hyperparameters that must be chosen by the user,
 including the hyperparameters in the regularization term,
 the maximum tree depth,
 and the number of trees in the ensemble.
 There are two additional hyperparameters that can be used to further prevent overfitting.
 First,
 one can use column or feature subsampling where each tree in the ensemble is built based on a (uniformly) random subset of all explanatory variables.
 This procedure can also speed up the algorithm because it reduces the number of covariates that must be searched across when creating new splits.
 Second,
 one can use shrinkage by scaling the predictions associated with each tree by a factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

,
 which is often called the learning rate.
 The predicted value of the outcome at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is then given by the sum of the previous prediction and the current prediction scaled by the learning rate.
 Shrinkage reduces the influence of each tree so that no individual tree has too much influence on the ensemble prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One possible approach to hyperparameter selection is to use a grid search where the user (1) specifies the domain of the hyperparameters,
 (2) applies the model to every combination of hyperparameters,
 and (3) selects the hyperparameters that perform the best in terms of some metric (e.g.,
 mean squared prediction error in the validation dataset).
 Grid search,
 however,
 is computationally inefficient because hyperparameter proposals are uninformed by previous evaluations.
 We instead use a Bayesian hyperparameter optimization procedure,
 which sequentially updates a probability model that relates the hyperparameter space to the performance metric and then uses the model to intelligently explore the hyperparameter space 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bergstra2011algorithms"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Specifically,
 our implementation of gradient boosting relies on the open-source library XGBoost,
 which we combine with the Hyperopt library that conducts Bayesian hyperparameter optimization using the tree-structured Parzen estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Traditional methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We benchmark the performance of the machine-learning approach against two traditional poverty-mapping methods:
 the unit-level and area-level models.
 Regarding unit-level models,
 the well-known methods of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 are based on the nested error model originally proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "battese1988asa"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This model assumes that (transformed) equivalized household income or consumption relates to a vector of household-level covariates according to the following model:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{iv}=x_{iv}\theta+\mu_{i}+\xi_{iv}\label{eq:sae-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{iv}$
\end_inset

 denotes the transformed equivalized income of household 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 in location 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iv}$
\end_inset

 represents a vector of household-specific characteristics,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is a vector of coefficients on the household characteristics,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 is location-specific random effect such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{\mu}^{2})$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{iv}$
\end_inset

 is a household-level error term such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{iv}\sim N(0,\sigma_{\xi}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The parameters of the model are typically estimated via restricted maximum likelihood or Henderson's Method III (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "henderson1953estimation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Henderson's Method III is coupled with generalized least squares to obtain the regression coefficients.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Once the model is estimated,
 the parameters are then used to repeatedly generate income estimates for all households in a corresponding census dataset.
 That is,
 the income level of each household is predicted 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 times using the model estimates as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{{w}}_{ivk}=x_{iv}\hat{\theta}+\mu_{ik}^{*}+\xi_{ivk}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 indexes replications and where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{ik}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{ivk}^{*}$
\end_inset

 are drawn from their assumed distributions.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a more detailed description.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 As a final step,
 the replicates can be used to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 estimates of the poverty rate for each geographic entity and these estimates can then be averaged to arrive at a final prediction for each area.
 Note that there are multiple versions of the unit-level model,
 distinguished primarily through different treatments of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{ik}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{ivk}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 We use a particular version known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

census empirical best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 method,
 which is a variant of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

empirical best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 method of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As opposed to empirical best estimates,
 census empirical best estimates do not include survey observations when calculating area-level poverty rates.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that when the sample size is small relative to the population,
 the difference between the two methods is negligible.
 For additional discussion,
 see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2019desagregacion"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key assumption of the unit-level model is that the distribution of the explanatory variables is similar between the survey used for estimation and the census used for prediction.
 This assumption is generally violated if the census is outdated – which is often the case in developing countries – and in this event the application of the unit-level model will lead to similarly outdated poverty maps 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "lange2018small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Importantly,
 machine-learning approaches to poverty mapping are commonly motivated by the fact that censuses are outdated and are thus viewed as a way to produce timely poverty maps in a data-constrained environment.
 Stated differently,
 given the differing data requirements between the unit-level model and the machine-learning approach,
 the two methods are not generally considered to be direct competitors.
 The unit-level model nevertheless serves as a useful performance benchmark,
 as it is often viewed as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

gold standard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of poverty mapping.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One closely related alternative,
 known as the unit-context model,
 was specifically developed for use in off-census years.
 Initially proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "nguyen2012method"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and further refined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "masaki2020small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 these models are based on a simple modification of the unit-level approach:
 rather than modeling household-level income as a function of household-level characteristics,
 unit-context models instead use only area-level covariates from the census.
 Specifically,
 the unit-context model replaces the survey-based covariates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iv}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sae-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) with census aggregates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and thus relaxes the assumption that the survey-based covariates match the moments of those from the census.
 The unit-context model is then a direct competitor to the machine-learning approach because both apply to off-census years.
 Recent research,
 however,
 has shown that the unit-context model often produces biased and noisy poverty estimates,
 largely due to its inability to explain between-household variation in income 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to these performance issues,
 the World Bank instead recommends using the area-level model in off-census years 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 First proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fay1979estimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 area-level models conduct estimation and prediction using data aggregated to the geographic area of interest.
 The area-level model consists of two basic parts.
 The first part of the model links the actual or true poverty rates 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 entity to a vector of area-level covariates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=x_{i}\beta+\varepsilon_{i}\label{eq:linking-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a vector of parameters to be estimated and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents random area-level effects that are assumed to be mean zero with a constant variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Since we lack information on the true poverty rates,
 this model cannot be immediately fit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead,
 direct estimates of poverty rates from survey data 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 are used as the outcome variable.
 These estimates are nevertheless subject to sampling error because they are based on the area-specific sample data.
 The second part of the model thus assumes that the direct estimates are centered around the true poverty rates as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\omega_{i}\label{eq:2nd stage-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the measurement error and is assumed to have heteroskedastic variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega,i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 because area sample sizes are often unequal.
 These variances are assumed to be known,
 although in practice they are estimated using the survey data.
 Given the two parts of the model,
 the survey data can then be used to obtain parameter estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The best linear unbiased predictor (BLUP) is obtained by predicting poverty rates through the model 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\hat{\beta}+\hat{\varepsilon}_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\varepsilon}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represent the estimated coefficients and area-level effects,
 respectively.
 More specifically,
 the estimated area effect is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\varepsilon}_{i}=\psi_{i}\left(y_{i}^{d}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}\right)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}=\hat{\sigma}_{\varepsilon}^{2}/\left(\hat{\sigma}_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\hat{\sigma}_{\omega,i}^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 In essence,
 the final estimates for areas contained in the survey are the weighted average between the direct estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and the synthetic estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 where the weights,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\psi_{i})$
\end_inset

,
 are determined by the quality of the model.
 For the non-sampled entities,
 the area-level model simply uses the synthetic estimator to produce predictions.
 As mentioned,
 a critical feature of the area-level model is that it is a direct competitor to the machine-learning approach because both methods are intended for use in off-census years.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary,
 we use both the unit-level and area-level models as benchmarks in our assessment of the modern approach to poverty mapping.
 While the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

gold standard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 unit-level model provides an absolute performance benchmark by approximating the upper performance bound,
 the area-level model provides a relative performance benchmark since it is the leading traditional competitor to the modern approach.
 In our implementations of these models,
 we rely exclusively on linear specifications because identifying covariate transformations (e.g.,
 quadratic or cubic terms) for each replication would substantially complicate our sampling experiments.
 To the extent that adding covariate transformations could improve the performance of the traditional methods,
 our experiments are thus favorable to the modern approach because gradient boosting benefits from automated model selection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model Validation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:validation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coefficient of determination or 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the most commonly used metric for validating poverty maps generated using machine-learning methods.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In this section,
 we first critically assess this validation approach by discussing three ways in which the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be misleading in terms of model assessment.
 We then motivate our simulation experiments by proposing an alternative approach that remedies many of the limitations of the conventional procedure.
 Specifically,
 we propose (1) assessing model performance relative to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 poverty measures rather than direct estimates,
 and (2) using mean squared error and targeting performance as the principal validation metrics.
 Note that our objective is to propose a validation procedure that is approximately ideal for our purposes and we recognize that it may not be feasible for all poverty-mapping exercises.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For example,
 our validation procedure requires a dataset that is sufficiently large for repeated sampling and such data is unlikely to be available in many contexts.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 denote the actual or true poverty indicator for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 geographical entity.
 Further,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denote the direct estimate and model-based prediction of the poverty indicator,
 respectively.
 The standard method for assessing the predictive performance of model-based poverty estimates calculates the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression like the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=\alpha+\delta y_{i}^{p}+\zeta_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 are the regression coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that any regression of this form can only be estimated for the sampled regions with direct estimates.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for the above regression can be written as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\zeta}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\label{eq:r2direct}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the direct estimate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\zeta}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 thus conveys information about how much of the variance of the direct estimate is explained by the model-based prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While unbiased,
 direct estimates can be imprecise estimates of true poverty indicators 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Ideally,
 one would assess the performance of model-based predictions on the basis of the true poverty indicators themselves.
 That is,
 model-based predictions are more appropriately assessed using the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the following regression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=\alpha+\delta y_{i}^{p}+\upsilon_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\upsilon$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for this regression can written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}\label{eq:r2true}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the true poverty indicator and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\upsilon$
\end_inset

.
 We are specifically interested in characterizing the bias of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when it is used to assess true quantity of interest,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is a mean-zero error term with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 we view this as a classical measurement error problem where the direct estimate of the poverty indicator is a noisy estimate of the true poverty indicator.
 Note that the preceding equation implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta_{i}=\upsilon_{i}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\zeta}^{2}=\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}+\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we then define the bias as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=R_{a}^{2}-R_{d}^{2}\,.\label{eq:bias}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting Eqs.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2direct"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) into our expression for the bias,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}-\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}(\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2})}{\sigma_{d}^{2}\sigma_{a}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}R_{a}^{2}\,.\label{eq:bias_res}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 we can substitute Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias_res"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) into Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and rearrange,
 which gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}\left(\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\right)\label{eq:relationship}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and establishes a basic relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for additional mathematical details.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:relationship"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) above,
 we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}=0$
\end_inset

 or,
 equivalently,
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In the presence of measurement error or when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}>0$
\end_inset

,
 we then see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will be biased downward and the magnitude of the bias will be determined by the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2})/\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Validation exercises based on 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will thus tend to mischaracterize the performance of poverty maps,
 with the resulting estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 being biased downward.
 One implication of this bias is that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is context-dependent and will generally be influenced by the design of the survey data (e.g.,
 larger sample sizes will tend to reduce measurement error,
 all else equal).
 In particular,
 a poorly performing model applied to a low bias setting may yield an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is higher than an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a strong model applied to a high bias setting.
 The presence of bias in the measures of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can thus reverse the rank ordering of models when comparisons are made across contexts.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for this type of cross-context comparison of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 values.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is an additional form of bias in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that can conversely lead to overoptimistic conclusions regarding model validity.
 To illustrate this,
 note that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is mathematically equivalent to the squared (Pearson) correlation coefficient between 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

.
 A well-known property of the correlation coefficient is that it is invariant to any positive affine transformation of its arguments,
 implying that the 
\shape italic
squared
\shape default
 correlation coefficient is invariant to 
\shape italic
any
\shape default
 affine transformation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gujarati2003basic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
More specifically,
 the correlation coefficient is invariant to any 
\shape italic
positive
\shape default
 affine transformation because it changes signs when one of its arguments is scaled by some negative constant.
 The 
\shape italic
squared
\shape default
 correlation coefficient is then invariant to any affine transformation.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We can thus write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},q_{1}+q_{2}y_{i}^{p})\label{eq:shift}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}$
\end_inset

 are some arbitrary constants.
 The implication of the above is that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is insensitive to systematic bias in the model-based poverty estimates.
 For example,
 consider some unbiased poverty estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

,
 meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{u}-y_{i}^{a})=0.$
\end_inset

 Now consider another estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 up to some locational shift,
 implying that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}=y_{i}^{u}+q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{b}-y_{i}^{a})=q$
\end_inset

.
 While 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 is biased (i.e.,
 it will systematically over- or under-estimate poverty),
 it will achieve an 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A final issue related to 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric is that it is affected by the variance of the true poverty measures.
 To see this,
 consider the following re-expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=1-\frac{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\alpha-\delta y_{i}^{p})^{2}}{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\bar{{y}}^{a})^{2}}=1-\frac{\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{a},\alpha+\delta y_{i}^{p})}{\text{{Var}}(y_{i}^{a})}\label{eq:variance}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of geographical entities.
 The above shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as a normalized version of the mean squared error with the variance of the true poverty indicator used as the normalization factor.
 This approach to normalization produces an additional source of context-dependence in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically,
 consider two models that perform equally well in that they achieve the same mean squared error.
 However,
 assume that one model is applied to a context where the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 is large and the other is applied to a low variance context.
 The above then implies that the model applied to the high variance context will achieve a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 even though the performance of the two models is arguably identical.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rather than relying on 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric,
 one can avoid regression-based model assessment altogether by calculating the mean squared error directly:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=\frac{{1}}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-y_{i}^{p})^{2}\,,\label{eq:mse}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which avoids the normalization issue and provides a more direct understanding of the expected squared error of the estimates.
 The mean squared error in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is sensitive to systematic biases in the predictions.
 Reconsidering the biased and unbiased poverty estimates described above (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

),
 we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{b},y_{i}^{a})=\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{u}+q,y_{i}^{a})=\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{u},y_{i}^{a})+q^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Systematic biases in the estimates will thus be penalized by the mean squared error.
 Further,
 with multiple estimates for each entity,
 one can calculate entity-specific mean squared errors,
 which can be decomposed into parts associated with the bias and variance of the estimates.
 Where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ik}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denotes estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,2,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 entity,
 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{{MSE}}_{i}=\frac{{1}}{K}\sum_{k}(y_{i}^{a}-y_{ik}^{p})^{2}=\frac{{1}}{K}\sum_{k}(y_{ik}^{p}-\bar{{y}}_{i}^{p})^{2}+(\bar{{y}}_{i}^{p}-y_{i}^{a})^{2}\label{eq:mseg}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first term on the right-hand side of the above captures the variance of the estimates for entity 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and the second term captures the (squared) bias.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "hastie2009elements"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for derivation and detailed discussion of the bias-variance decomposition.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary reason for obtaining granular poverty estimates is to improve the targeting of resources.
 While understanding the error of the poverty estimates is useful in this regard,
 the ultimate concern is to understand how different methodological choices affect poverty alleviation by influencing the efficiency of targeting.
 Consequently,
 we also consider how machine-learning methods perform in terms of reducing poverty in the context of the census created from the Mexican Intercensal Survey.
 Similar to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we propose simulating a hypothetical poverty-alleviation program that distributes resources to households according to a given set of small area estimates.
