Bayesian additive regression trees perform slightly better than gradient boosting,
 but the performance gains are not large.
 For example,
 in terms of targeting performance,
 gradient boosting achieves a median poverty rate of 20.29 whereas the Bayesian method achieves a median rate of 20.26.
 
